1
00:00:00,420 --> 00:00:02,415
Book Four, Proposition Eleven.

2
00:00:02,415 --> 00:00:07,070
In a given circle to inscribe an equilateral and equiangular pentagon.

3
00:00:07,070 --> 00:00:15,450
Let A B C D E be the given circle; it is required to inscribe in the circle A B C D E an equilateral and equiangular pentagon.

4
00:00:15,870 --> 00:00:26,437
By Four Ten, set out the golden isosceles triangle F G H having each of its base angles at G and H double the vertex angle at F.

5
00:00:26,437 --> 00:00:40,306
By Four Two, inscribe in circle A B C D E the triangle A C D equiangular with triangle F G H, so that each of the base angles A C D and C D A is double the angle C A D.

6
00:00:40,306 --> 00:00:49,800
By One Nine, bisect angles A C D and C D A respectively by the chords C E and D B, and join A B, B C, D E, and E A.

7
00:00:50,220 --> 00:01:07,422
Because each of the angles A C D and C D A is double angle C A D and has been bisected by C E and D B, the five inscribed angles D A C, A C E, E C D, C D B, and B D A are all equal to one another.

8
00:01:07,422 --> 00:01:28,135
By Three Twenty-Six, equal inscribed angles stand on equal arcs, so the five arcs A B, B C, C D, D E, and E A are equal; and by Three Twenty-Nine, equal arcs are subtended by equal chords, so sides A B, B C, C D, D E, and E A are equal.

9
00:01:28,135 --> 00:01:32,348
Therefore the pentagon A B C D E is equilateral.

10
00:01:32,348 --> 00:01:42,177
Next, because arc A B equals arc D E, add arc B C D to each: the whole arc A B C D equals the whole arc E D C B.

11
00:01:42,177 --> 00:02:00,433
By Three Twenty-Seven, the inscribed angles A E D and B A E standing on those arcs are equal, and by the same reasoning all five interior angles B A E, A B C, B C D, C D E, and A E D are equal to one another.

12
00:02:00,433 --> 00:02:11,140
Therefore an equilateral and equiangular pentagon A B C D E has been inscribed in the given circle, as was required to do.

13
00:02:11,560 --> 00:02:16,761
Proposition Four Eleven crowns the Pythagorean theory of regular polygons in the circle.

14
00:02:16,761 --> 00:02:33,367
As Heath remarks against De Morgan, rather than stepping off a decagon around the circumference, Euclid inscribes the golden triangle A C D and bisects its base angles with chords C E and D B—tracing four of the five lines of the Pythagorean pentagram-star right inside the circle.

15
00:02:33,367 --> 00:02:44,773
Each of the five thirty-six-degree inscribed angles intercepts one-fifth of the circumference, while each interior pentagon angle spans three-fifths of the circle, or one hundred eight degrees.

16
00:02:44,773 --> 00:03:00,080
In the GEOMETOR G-Index, node G one hundred sixty-three unites twelve direct rules across one hundred eight ancestor nodes, directly enabling the circumscribed pentagon in Four Twelve, the fifteen-gon in Four Sixteen, and the Platonic solids of Book Thirteen.

17
